package claudecode

import (
	providerspi "github.com/openbox-ai/openbox-shift-left/internal/provider"
)

// Capabilities returns the Claude Code adapter's capability profile.
func Capabilities() []providerspi.Capability {
	return []providerspi.Capability{
		{Key: "identity.register", Supported: true, How: "agent/create via `openbox init`; provider-independent"},
		{Key: "telemetry.hook", Supported: true, How: "the 11 original lifecycle/tool hooks (SessionStart/UserPromptSubmit/Pre+PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure/Stop/SubagentStop/SubagentStart/PermissionDenied/StopFailure/SessionEnd), plus 21 observe-only lifecycle signals covering setup, instructions, prompt expansion, message display, permission requests, tool batches, notifications, tasks, teammates, config changes, cwd changes, directory additions, file changes, worktree removal, compaction and model switching → normalized events. `WorktreeCreate` is deliberately NOT registered: its stdout last line IS the created worktree path, so a registered observer that emits nothing would break `claude --worktree`; the ceiling is 32 of 33 documented Claude Code hooks, and it is a choice. Every one of these hooks, old and new, is registered by the installer, so an EXISTING install does not fire them until `openbox init` is re-run; an older Claude Code that does not know the keys never invokes them and the events are simply absent (fail-open). Nothing in this landing schedules that re-run: a machine that upgrades this binary without re-running `init` keeps observing only its pre-upgrade hook set until a human runs it"},
		{Key: "tool.events", Supported: true, How: "PreToolUse/PostToolUse over all tools incl. mcp__* (matcher \"*\"), plus PostToolUseFailure for the failure half; the two post-hooks are mutually exclusive per call, which is what makes the outcome structural"},
		{Key: "tool.status", Supported: true, How: "status completed|failed on every tool ActivityCompleted, derived from WHICH hook fired; PostToolUse is documented and verified to fire only after a successful tool, PostToolUseFailure only after a failure. Never parsed from tool output; not content-gated, so it ships identically with content_capture off. metadata.is_interrupt (tri-state) separates a user cancellation from a real tool failure; both are `failed`, and an operator needs to tell them apart"},
		{Key: "commit.binding", Supported: true, How: "OpenBox-Session git trailer; provider-independent"},
		{Key: "telemetry.tokens", Supported: true, How: "PER TURN (ResolveFinops, default on): Stop/SubagentStop → llm_completion activity pair carrying model + 4 token counts, plus the SessionEnd session rollup. Window sums, not per-model-call; hooks fire per turn. Transcript projection binds one egressing string (model), allowlist-enforced (INV-2); off the hot path. Cost never derived here"},
		{Key: "telemetry.model", Supported: true, How: "per-turn model attribution from the transcript's message.model (last non-empty in the window); omitted rather than guessed when a window names none. SessionStart's model field is separate and not guaranteed present"},
		{Key: "verdict.apply", Supported: true, How: "enforce leg: every gated PreToolUse call AND every UserPromptSubmit prompt is evaluated by /evaluate → permissionDecision:deny on tools, decision:block on prompts; ON by default; tighten-only, never emits allow as a grant; REQUIRE_APPROVAL is held for a real decision and denies/blocks if unanswered. Never `ask`: rendering the provider's own prompt would ask the developer to approve their own filed request (OD-E9-1), so the hold decides and this adapter renders the outcome. A HALT the control plane returns additionally stops the session: continue:false now, and a local latch refuses every later prompt/tool call in that run (a `--resume`d continuation starts unlatched and is re-evaluated fresh). Prompt gating and the raised UserPromptSubmit timeout are installer-registered; an existing install needs `openbox init` re-run. ConfigChange (settings-file edits) is gated too, except `source:policy_settings`, which the provider documents as unblockable and this adapter never sends to /evaluate (a raw compare, not the enum fallback) — and, separately, the provider never runs the hook at all for server-managed settings, so that second path is invisible to the gate by construction. An armed denial renders decision:'block' (there is no ask verb for a settings edit); REQUIRE_APPROVAL cannot be held on a signal row — no activity_id exists for an approval to attach to — so it waits the approval budget (~30s) and is then denied, exactly as UserPromptSubmit behaves today. ConfigChange shares the raised 30s ceiling with PreToolUse/UserPromptSubmit so a settings-edit decision is never lost to too tight a budget"},
		{Key: "enforce.rewrite", Supported: true, How: "local secret redaction → permissionDecision:allow + updatedInput on the proceed path (Write/Edit bodies); allow rides only a redacting rewrite; gated on content posture"},
	}
}
